; mold: which aircraft flies which leg, found by a search over a network
; of actors that grows as it's needed, like a slime mold. An experiment:
; can constraint propagation, as a CP-SAT solver does it, be actors
; sending each other messages, and what does that cost beside the same
; thing as a value and a loop?
;
;     node bin/slightc.ts --target x86_64 -o mold examples/mold.slight
;     SLIGHT_CLOCK=virtual ./mold              (reads examples/mold-day.sexp)
;     SLIGHT_CLOCK=virtual ./mold day.sexp
;     ./mold --values day.sexp                 (the mold as a value: no actors)
;     SLIGHT_CLOCK=virtual ./mold --split 2 day.sexp
;                                              (the first two choices each a
;                                              process, the islands shared out
;                                              among them)
;     ./mold --settle system day.sexp          (ask :system whether the mold is
;                                              quiet, in place of the clock)
;     ./mold --best ...                        (only the cheapest: a branch
;                                              that can't beat the best known
;                                              is left)
;     ./mold --stats ...                       (and what the search did)
; args: --split 2
;
; bench/mold.py runs it with sets of these, and times them, and
; t/models/mold.py makes days, and counts their assignments another way:
; examples/mold.expected is its, and t/392 to t/396's, which run the
; other ways of keeping the mold, and --best.
;
; The day, in examples/mold-day.sexp: aircraft, each with where it
; starts, the minutes it may fly before maintenance and what a minute of
; it costs; and legs, each from an airport to another, leaving and
; landing at a minute. Every leg wants an aircraft. An aircraft flies its
; legs in order, each from where the last landed, no sooner than the turn
; after it, and their minutes within its own. The program counts the
; ways, and finds the cheapest.
;
; The model is the one tail assignment is usually given: a leg doesn't
; choose an aircraft but its predecessor, the leg its aircraft flew
; before it, or an aircraft's start. Where and when a leg leaves says
; which those can be, before anything's decided, so the places and times
; are in the choices and only two rules are left:
;
; - a predecessor is taken once (two legs can't both follow one);
; - an aircraft's minutes, added up along its line, stay within its own.
;
; A node is an aircraft's start or a leg, and each is an actor, once
; something sends to it. Its state is (dom pred tail mins next): the
; predecessors it could still have, the one it has (or -1), its aircraft
; and its line's minutes so far (once its line reaches back to a start),
; and the node that took it (or -1). Four messages go between them:
;
;     (:assign p)    you follow p         from the search, or itself
;     (:take j)      j follows you        to a predecessor
;     (:drop p)      you can't follow p   to the others that could have
;     (:line a m)    your line is a's,    down a line, as it's learnt
;                    m minutes so far
;
; and a node left with one predecessor takes it, which drops it for
; others, and so on: that's the propagation, and nothing runs it but the
; mailboxes. A node tells the search what it needs to know, (:assigned
; k), (:line k a) and (:conflict k), and that's all the search sees of
; what the rules do.
;
; What each node does with a message is one function, node-step, of its
; state and the message, giving its new state and what to send. So the
; same rules run two ways: as actors (node), and, with --values, as a
; loop over a tuple of states with a list of messages to work through
; (v-run). The search is the same for both, and so are its decisions.
;
; The search (search, try): take the first leg without a predecessor,
; and for each it could have, decide it, wait till the mold is quiet,
; and go on from there, or turn back at a conflict.
;
; - Quiet: on the virtual clock an island's time moves only when nothing
;   on it can run, so a timer a millisecond off rings exactly when the
;   mold has settled. (Hence SLIGHT_CLOCK=virtual.) --settle system asks
;   the :system device instead: every node waiting with nothing in its
;   mailbox. That works on the real clock too, and costs a message for
;   each process on the island each time it's asked.
; - Turning back: each node keeps the states it had before each level of
;   the search changed it, and (:undo level) puts it back; it tells the
;   search, (:changed k), the first time a level changes it, so only
;   those are told. Nothing's copied: the states are values. With
;   --values there's nothing to undo, the old tuple being still there.
; - Splitting (--split d): the first d choices aren't tried in turn but
;   each given to a process of its own (branch). A search has a range of
;   the islands, all of them at first, and shares it out evenly among
;   the branches it starts, each running on the first island of its
;   share, so the branches of branches spread over every island.
;   The parent asks its nodes for their states, and that tuple, the mold
;   as a value, is what's copied; the child grows nodes of its own from
;   it as it touches them, and never turns back past where it began.
;   A node is named by its number, never its pid, so a copy needs no
;   rewiring: a node asks the search whose it is for a pid, once.
;
; - The bound (--best): the search wants only the cheapest, so it leaves
;   a branch that can't come to less than the best known. What a branch
;   must come to at least (lower): what its legs with an aircraft cost,
;   and the minutes of the rest flown by the cheapest aircraft with
;   minutes left, a minute at a time, as if a leg could be shared out;
;   no real assignment is cheaper. It tries the cheapest predecessor
;   first, so a good one is known early. A process (keeper) holds the
;   best any search has found: each tells it, and asks it when it starts
;   and after each thousand decisions, since searches on other islands
;   find things too. The best is the same however it's found; the
;   decisions it takes aren't, with more than one search.
;
; What it found is in docs/PLAN.md, "A mold of constraints".

; --- the day ------------------------------------------------------------------

(defun reading (f records)
    (match (read f)
        ((:sexp _ r) (reading f (cons r records)))
        ((:eof _)    (disconnect f) (reverse records))))

(defun read-records (path)
    (match (open :fs/read path)
        ((:ok f)         (send f (list :read :sexp)) (reading f ()))
        ((:error reason) (raise (list :no-day path reason)))))

(defun aircraft? (r) (match r ((:aircraft _ _ _ _) #true) (_ #false)))
(defun leg?      (r) (match r ((:leg _ _ _ _ _)    #true) (_ #false)))
(defun leaves    (l) (match l ((:leg _ _ _ dep _)  dep)))
(defun block     (l) (match l ((:leg _ _ _ dep arr) (- arr dep))))

(defun number (from xs) (zip (range from (+ from (length xs))) xs))

; The nodes a leg could follow: the starts of the aircraft that are where
; it leaves from and could fly it at all, and the legs that land there a
; turn or more before it leaves.
(defun before (turn planes legs leg)
    (match leg
        ((:leg _ from _ dep arr)
            (append
                (map car (filter (lambda (p) (match p ((_ (:aircraft _ start limit _))
                                                 (and (eq? start from) (<= (- arr dep) limit)))))
                                 planes))
                (map car (filter (lambda (l) (match l ((_ (:leg _ _ to _ landed))
                                                 (and (eq? to from) (<= (+ landed turn) dep)))))
                                 legs))))))

; The day as the search has it, #(planes legs statics rates initial
; cheapest minutes): how many aircraft and legs; for each node (the
; aircraft's starts, then the legs in the order they leave) what never
; changes, (minutes after limits), after being the legs that could
; follow it, each (node minutes), and limits the aircraft's minutes;
; what a minute of each aircraft costs; each node's state before
; anything's decided; the aircraft, cheapest first, each (aircraft rate
; limit); and the minutes of all the legs together.
(defun day (records)
    (let turn (fold/l 0 (lambda (t r) (match r ((:turn m) m) (_ t))) records))
    (let planes (number 0 (filter aircraft? records)))
    (let m (length planes))
    (let legs (number m (sort-by leaves (filter leg? records))))
    (let limits (list->tuple (map (lambda (p) (match p ((_ (:aircraft _ _ limit _)) limit))) planes)))
    (let could (map (lambda (l) (list (car l) (before turn planes legs (cadr l)) (block (cadr l)))) legs))
    (let after (lambda (k) (map (lambda (c) (list (car c) (caddr c)))
                                (filter (lambda (c) (member? k (cadr c))) could))))
    (tuple m (length legs)
        (list->tuple (append
            (map (lambda (p) (list 0 (after (car p)) limits)) planes)
            (map (lambda (c) (list (caddr c) (after (car c)) limits)) could)))
        (list->tuple (map (lambda (p) (match p ((_ (:aircraft _ _ _ rate)) rate))) planes))
        (list->tuple (append
            (map (lambda (p) (list () (car p) (car p) 0 -1)) planes)
            (map (lambda (c) (list (cadr c) -1 -1 0 -1)) could)))
        (sort-by cadr (map (lambda (p) (match p ((a (:aircraft _ _ limit rate)) (list a rate limit)))) planes))
        (sum (map caddr could))))

(defun planes  (inst)   (tuple/get inst 0))
(defun legs    (inst)   (tuple/get inst 1))
(defun static  (inst k) (tuple/get (tuple/get inst 2) k))
(defun rate    (inst a) (tuple/get (tuple/get inst 3) a))
(defun initial (inst)   (tuple/get inst 4))
(defun cheapest (inst)  (tuple/get inst 5))
(defun minutes (inst)   (tuple/get inst 6))

; --- a node's rules -----------------------------------------------------------

; What node k does with a message: (state sends), its new state (the old
; one itself if nothing changed) and what it sends, each (to message), to
; being a node, or -1 for the search.
(defun node-step (k static st msg)
    (match msg
        ((:assign p) (step-assign k st p))
        ((:drop p)   (step-drop k st p))
        ((:take j)   (step-take k static st j))
        ((:line a m) (step-line k static st a m))))

(defun conflict (k st) (list st (list (list -1 (list :conflict k)))))

(defun step-assign (k st p)
    (match st
        ((dom pred tail mins next)
            (cond
                ((== pred p)           (list st ()))
                ((!= pred -1)          (conflict k st))
                ((not (member? p dom)) (conflict k st))
                (#true
                    (list (list (list p) p tail mins next)
                          (list (list p (list :take k)) (list -1 (list :assigned k)))))))))

; Left with one it could follow, a node follows it.
(defun step-drop (k st p)
    (match st
        ((dom pred tail mins next)
            (cond
                ((not (member? p dom)) (list st ()))
                ((== pred p)           (conflict k st))
                (#true
                    (let left (remove (lambda (q) (== q p)) dom))
                    (match left
                        (()  (conflict k st))
                        ((q) (step-assign k (list left pred tail mins next) q))
                        (_   (list (list left pred tail mins next) ()))))))))

; Taken, a node is no one else's to follow, and passes on its line if it
; knows it.
(defun step-take (k static st j)
    (match st
        ((dom pred tail mins next)
            (cond
                ((== next j)  (list st ()))
                ((!= next -1) (conflict k st))
                (#true
                    (let drops (map (lambda (s) (list (car s) (list :drop k)))
                                    (filter (lambda (s) (!= (car s) j)) (cadr static))))
                    (list (list dom pred tail mins j)
                          (if (== tail -1) drops (cons (list j (list :line tail mins)) drops))))))))

; Its line learnt, a node passes it on, or, with no one after it yet,
; drops itself for the legs that would take the aircraft past its minutes.
(defun step-line (k static st a m)
    (match st
        ((dom pred _ _ next)
            (match static
                ((minutes after limits)
                    (let mine (+ m minutes))
                    (let limit (tuple/get limits a))
                    (if (> mine limit)
                        (conflict k st)
                        (list (list dom pred a mine next)
                              (cons (list -1 (list :line k a))
                                    (if (!= next -1)
                                        (list (list next (list :line a mine)))
                                        (map (lambda (s) (list (car s) (list :drop k)))
                                             (filter (lambda (s) (> (+ mine (cadr s)) limit)) after)))))))))))

; Where a search is, (assigned cost left used tails): the legs with a
; predecessor, a bit each; what the legs whose aircraft is known cost;
; the minutes of the legs whose aircraft isn't; and, when it's the
; cheapest that's wanted, () otherwise, the minutes each aircraft has
; flown, and each node's aircraft, or -1.
(defun on-assigned (inst at k)
    (match at
        ((assigned cost left used tails)
            (list (bit/or assigned (bit/shl 1 (- k (planes inst)))) cost left used tails))))

(defun on-line (inst at k a)
    (match at
        ((assigned cost left used tails)
            (let d (car (static inst k)))
            (list assigned (+ cost (* d (rate inst a))) (- left d)
                  (if (nil? used) () (tuple/set used a (+ (tuple/get used a) d)))
                  (if (nil? tails) () (tuple/set tails k a))))))

; What the search keeps of what the nodes tell it, (at bad changed):
; where it is; whether any node found a conflict; and the nodes, actors
; only, whose states this level changed, to put back.
(defun note (inst acc msg)
    (match acc
        ((at bad changed)
            (match msg
                ((:assigned k) (list (on-assigned inst at k) bad changed))
                ((:line k a)   (list (on-line inst at k a) bad changed))
                ((:conflict _) (list at #true changed))
                ((:changed k)  (list at bad (cons k changed)))))))

; --- the mold as a value ------------------------------------------------------

; The messages worked through, newest first, till there are none or a
; node finds a conflict: (states acc).
(defun v-run (inst states queue acc)
    (match queue
        (() (list states acc))
        (((to msg) & rest)
            (if (== to -1)
                (match (note inst acc msg)
                    ((at bad _) (if bad (list states (list at bad ())) (v-run inst states rest (list at bad ())))))
                (match (node-step to (static inst to) (tuple/get states to) msg)
                    ((st sends)
                        (v-run inst
                               (if (eq? st (tuple/get states to)) states (tuple/set states to st))
                               (append sends rest)
                               acc)))))))

; --- the mold as actors -------------------------------------------------------

; A node: its number, what never changes for it, the search it's part
; of, its state, the states it had before each level changed it, newest
; first, each (level state), and the pids of the nodes it has sent to.
; The first time a level changes it, it tells the search, which is how
; the search knows whom to tell to put it back.
(defun node (k static search st undo known)
    (recv
        ((:msg level m)
            (match (node-step k static st m)
                ((st2 sends)
                    (if (or (eq? st2 st) (and (cons? undo) (>= (caar undo) level)))
                        (node k static search st2 undo (deliver search level sends known))
                        (do (send search (list :changed k))
                            (node k static search st2 (cons (list level st) undo)
                                  (deliver search level sends known)))))))
        ((:undo level)
            (match (unwind undo level st)
                ((st2 undo2) (node k static search st2 undo2 known))))
        ((:state to)
            (reply to st)
            (node k static search st undo known))))

(defun unwind (undo level st)
    (match undo
        (((l was) & rest) (if (>= l level) (unwind rest level was) (list st undo)))
        (()               (list st undo))))

; Sends each, asking the search for the pid of a node not sent to before
; (which grows it, if it wasn't there): the pids known after.
(defun deliver (search level sends known)
    (match sends
        (() known)
        (((to msg) & rest)
            (if (== to -1)
                (do (send search msg)
                    (deliver search level rest known))
                (match (lookup to known)
                    (:not-found
                        (let pid (ask search (list :who to)))
                        (send pid (list :msg level msg))
                        (deliver search level rest (cons (list to pid) known)))
                    (pid
                        (send pid (list :msg level msg))
                        (deliver search level rest known)))))))

; The search's side of a mold of actors, #(pids mold sys): the pid of
; each node that's grown, or (); the state each of the others would
; start from; and the :system device, if that's how it's asked whether
; the mold is quiet.
(defun grow (inst world k)
    (match world
        (#(pids mold sys)
            (if (pid? (tuple/get pids k))
                world
                (tuple (tuple/set pids k (fork node k (static inst k) $$ (tuple/get mold k) () ()))
                       mold sys)))))

(defun pid-of (world k) (tuple/get (tuple/get world 0) k))
(defun grown  (world)   (filter pid? (tuple->list (tuple/get world 0))))

; Waits till the mold is quiet, answering the nodes that ask for a pid
; and keeping what they tell: (world acc).
(defun settle (inst world acc)
    (recv
        ((:who k to)
            (let w (grow inst world k))
            (reply to (pid-of w k))
            (settle inst w acc))
        (:settled (list world acc))
        (msg      (settle inst world (note inst acc msg)))))

; The same by asking :system: what's waiting for the search is taken, up
; to a mark it sends itself; then the island's processes are asked for,
; and the mold is quiet if the search has nothing waiting and each of its
; nodes waits with nothing either. If not, the others get a turn first.
(defun settle/system (inst world acc)
    (send $$ :mark)
    (match (drain inst world acc)
        ((w a)
            (let sys (tuple/get w 2))
            (send sys (list :processes (island)))
            (if (quiet? sys $$ #true)
                (list w a)
                (yield (settle/system inst w a))))))

(defun drain (inst world acc)
    (recv
        ((:who k to)
            (let w (grow inst world k))
            (reply to (pid-of w k))
            (drain inst w acc))
        (:mark (list world acc))
        (msg   (drain inst world (note inst acc msg)))))

(defun quiet? (sys me so-far)
    (match (read sys)
        ((:end _) so-far)
        ((:process _ info)
            (quiet? sys me
                (and so-far
                     (cond
                         ((eq? (lookup :pid info) me)    (== (lookup :mail info) 0))
                         ((eq? (lookup :parent info) me) (and (eq? (lookup :state info) :recv)
                                                              (== (lookup :mail info) 0)))
                         (#true                          #true)))))))

; --- the search ---------------------------------------------------------------

; What's the same whichever way the mold is kept: cx is #(inst actors
; split system from to keeper), the day, whether the mold is actors, how
; many choices deep the search splits, whether :system says when it's
; quiet, the islands that are this search's to share out, from one up
; to but not including another, in 4096ths of an island, and the
; process that keeps the best cost found, or () when every assignment
; is wanted. A world is a mold: a tuple of states, or the search's side
; of the actors.

(defun fresh (cx mold)
    (if (tuple/get cx 1)
        (tuple (tuple/make (tuple/len mold) ()) mold
               (if (tuple/get cx 3) (match (open :system) ((:ok sys) sys)) ()))
        mold))

; Node j told to follow v, and the mold left to settle: (world acc).
(defun decide (cx world j v level acc)
    (let inst (tuple/get cx 0))
    (cond
        ((not (tuple/get cx 1))
            (v-run inst world (list (list j (list :assign v))) acc))
        (#true
            (let w (grow inst world j))
            (send (pid-of w j) (list :msg level (list :assign v)))
            (if (tuple/get cx 3)
                (settle/system inst w acc)
                (do (after 1 $$ :settled)
                    (settle inst w acc))))))

; The world as it was before a level: the tuple from before it, or the
; actors it changed told to put back what they had.
(defun retract (cx was world level changed)
    (if (tuple/get cx 1)
        (do (map (lambda (k) (send (pid-of world k) (list :undo level))) changed)
            world)
        was))

; What node j could still follow.
(defun domain (cx world j)
    (cond
        ((not (tuple/get cx 1))  (car (tuple/get world j)))
        ((pid? (pid-of world j)) (car (ask (pid-of world j) (list :state))))
        (#true                   (car (tuple/get (tuple/get world 1) j)))))

; And in the order to try them: as they come, or, when it's the cheapest
; that's wanted, those on the cheapest aircraft first, and those whose
; aircraft isn't known yet last.
(defun choices (cx world at j)
    (let dom (domain cx world j))
    (match at
        ((_ _ _ _ tails)
            (if (nil? tails)
                dom
                (sort-by (lambda (p)
                             (if (== (tuple/get tails p) -1)
                                 1000000
                                 (rate (tuple/get cx 0) (tuple/get tails p))))
                         dom)))))

; The mold as a value, to start a branch from: the tuple itself, or each
; grown node asked for its state.
(defun snapshot (cx world)
    (if (tuple/get cx 1)
        (fold/l (tuple/get world 1)
                (lambda (mold k)
                    (if (pid? (pid-of world k))
                        (tuple/set mold k (ask (pid-of world k) (list :state)))
                        mold))
                (range 0 (tuple/len (tuple/get world 1))))
        world))

; A search's nodes ended with it: how many it grew.
(defun finish (cx world)
    (if (tuple/get cx 1)
        (do (map kill (grown world))
            (if (tuple/get cx 3) (disconnect (tuple/get world 2)) ())
            (length (grown world)))
        0))

; A result is (assignments best decisions conflicts bounded forks
; nodes), best being the least cost, or -1 with no assignment, and
; bounded the branches left because they couldn't beat the best known.
(defun least (a b) (cond ((== a -1) b) ((== b -1) a) (#true (math/min a b))))
(defun add (x y)
    (match x ((c1 b1 d1 x1 l1 f1 n1)
        (match y ((c2 b2 d2 x2 l2 f2 n2)
            (list (+ c1 c2) (least b1 b2) (+ d1 d2) (+ x1 x2) (+ l1 l2) (+ f1 f2) (+ n1 n2)))))))

; --- the bound ----------------------------------------------------------------

; The least a search could still come to: what's on aircraft already,
; and the minutes that aren't, each on the cheapest aircraft with
; minutes left; -1 if the aircraft haven't the minutes between them.
(defun lower (order used left cost)
    (cond
        ((<= left 0)  cost)
        ((nil? order) -1)
        (#true
            (match (car order)
                ((a rate limit)
                    (let room (math/min left (- limit (tuple/get used a))))
                    (lower (cdr order) used (- left room) (+ cost (* room rate))))))))

; Whether to leave a branch: it can't come to less than the best known.
(defun cut? (cx at bound)
    (if (pid? (tuple/get cx 6))
        (match at
            ((_ cost left used _)
                (let low (lower (cheapest (tuple/get cx 0)) used left cost))
                (or (== low -1) (and (!= bound -1) (>= low bound)))))
        #false))

; The best cost any search has found, or -1.
(defun keeper (best)
    (recv
        ((:found cost) (keeper (least best cost)))
        ((:best to)    (reply to best) (keeper best))))

; The bound for what's tried next: the least of what was known and what
; the last choice came to; and, after a thousand decisions or more with
; other searches about, of what the keeper has heard since.
(defun heard (cx bound r)
    (let known (least bound (cadr r)))
    (if (and (pid? (tuple/get cx 6)) (and (> (tuple/get cx 2) 0) (>= (caddr r) 1000)))
        (least known (ask (tuple/get cx 6) (list :best)))
        known))

(defun first-clear (bits i)
    (if (== (bit/and (bit/shr bits i) 1) 0) i (first-clear bits (+ i 1))))

(defun ok (joined) (match joined ((:ok r) r) ((:error reason) (raise reason))))

; Every assignment from a settled world on, or every one that beats the
; bound: (world result).
(defun search (cx world at bound depth)
    (let inst (tuple/get cx 0))
    (cond
        ((== (car at) (- (bit/shl 1 (legs inst)) 1))
            (when (pid? (tuple/get cx 6))
                (send (tuple/get cx 6) (list :found (cadr at))))
            (list world (list 1 (cadr at) 0 0 0 0 0)))
        (#true
            (let j (+ (planes inst) (first-clear (car at) 0)))
            (let dom (choices cx world at j))
            (cond
                ((< depth (tuple/get cx 2))
                    (let mold (snapshot cx world))
                    (let share (div (- (tuple/get cx 5) (tuple/get cx 4)) (length dom)))
                    (let pids (map (lambda (iv)
                                       (let from (+ (tuple/get cx 4) (* (car iv) share)))
                                       (fork/on (div from 4096) branch
                                                (tuple/set (tuple/set cx 4 from) 5 (+ from share))
                                                mold at bound depth j (cadr iv)))
                                   (zip (range 0 (length dom)) dom)))
                    (list world (fold/l (list 0 -1 0 0 0 (length dom) 0) add (map ok (map join pids)))))
                (#true
                    (try cx world at bound depth j dom (list 0 -1 0 0 0 0 0)))))))

; Node j following each of dom in turn, and all that comes of each.
(defun try (cx world at bound depth j dom sum)
    (match dom
        (() (list world sum))
        ((v & rest)
            (match (decide cx world j v (+ depth 1) (list at #false ()))
                ((w (at2 bad changed))
                    (match (cond
                               (bad                 (list w (list 0 -1 0 1 0 0 0)))
                               ((cut? cx at2 bound) (list w (list 0 -1 0 0 1 0 0)))
                               (#true               (search cx w at2 bound (+ depth 1))))
                        ((w2 r)
                            (try cx (retract cx world w2 (+ depth 1) changed) at (heard cx bound r)
                                 depth j rest (add sum (add r (list 0 -1 1 0 0 0 0)))))))))))

; A process for one choice: a mold of its own, from its parent's as a
; value, and all that comes of node j following v, against the best
; known as it starts.
(defun branch (cx mold at bound depth j v)
    (let known (if (pid? (tuple/get cx 6))
                   (least bound (ask (tuple/get cx 6) (list :best)))
                   bound))
    (match (try cx (fresh cx mold) at known depth j (list v) (list 0 -1 0 0 0 0 0))
        ((w r) (add r (list 0 -1 0 0 0 0 (finish cx w))))))

; --- the program --------------------------------------------------------------

(defun main (o clock)
    (match o
        (#(path actors split system stats best)
            (let inst (day (read-records path)))
            (cond
                ((> (legs inst) 62)
                    (raise (list :too-many-legs (legs inst))))
                ((and actors (and (not system) (ne? clock "virtual")))
                    (raise "the clock says when the mold is quiet: run with SLIGHT_CLOCK=virtual, or --settle system"))
                (#true
                    (let cx (tuple inst actors split system 0 (* (islands) 4096)
                                   (if best (fork keeper -1) ())))
                    (let at (list 0 0 (minutes inst)
                                  (if best (tuple/make (planes inst) 0) ())
                                  (if best (list->tuple (map caddr (tuple->list (initial inst)))) ())))
                    (tty/write (legs inst) " legs, " (planes inst) " aircraft\n")
                    (match (search cx (fresh cx (initial inst)) at -1 0)
                        ((w (count least-cost decisions conflicts bounded forks nodes))
                            (when (not best) (tty/write count " assignments\n"))
                            (tty/write (if (== least-cost -1) "no best" (str "best " least-cost)) "\n")
                            (when stats
                                (tty/write "decisions " decisions ", conflicts " conflicts
                                           ", bounded " bounded ", forks " forks
                                           ", nodes " (+ nodes (finish cx w)) "\n"))
                            (when best (kill (tuple/get cx 6)))
                            ())))))))

(defun or-0 (v) (if (int? v) v 0))

(defun options (args o clock)
    (match args
        (()                         (main o clock))
        (("--values" & rest)        (options rest (tuple/set o 1 #false) clock))
        (("--split" n & rest)       (options rest (tuple/set o 2 (or-0 (str->int n))) clock))
        (("--settle" "system" & rest) (options rest (tuple/set o 3 #true) clock))
        (("--settle" "clock" & rest)  (options rest (tuple/set o 3 #false) clock))
        (("--stats" & rest)         (options rest (tuple/set o 4 #true) clock))
        (("--best" & rest)          (options rest (tuple/set o 5 #true) clock))
        ((p & rest)                 (options rest (tuple/set o 0 p) clock))))

(options @ARGV (tuple "examples/mold-day.sexp" #true 0 #false #false #false) (lookup "SLIGHT_CLOCK" %ENV))
